WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.480 --> 00:00:02.610
- [Narrator] From Peter the
Great to Vladimir Putin,

00:00:02.610 --> 00:00:04.680
Russian leaders have
attempted to modernize

00:00:04.680 --> 00:00:06.810
and mold the nation's judicial system.

00:00:06.810 --> 00:00:09.930
But legal and political experts
say that time after time,

00:00:09.930 --> 00:00:11.730
they've undermined the system by using it

00:00:11.730 --> 00:00:13.950
to wage war on their political opponents.

00:00:13.950 --> 00:00:15.420
- When they really care about something,

00:00:15.420 --> 00:00:18.000
they have no trouble using
the laws of blunt instrument,

00:00:18.000 --> 00:00:20.790
forbidding criticism of the war,

00:00:20.790 --> 00:00:25.020
forbidding people from gathering
in numbers on the street.

00:00:25.020 --> 00:00:27.930
All those kinds of things are
very, very crude instruments.

00:00:27.930 --> 00:00:29.130
- [Narrator] Charges like espionage

00:00:29.130 --> 00:00:32.010
and treason are among the
most powerful weapons.

00:00:32.010 --> 00:00:35.040
In recent years, former
US Marine, Paul Whelan,

00:00:35.040 --> 00:00:36.690
basketball player, Brittney Griner,

00:00:36.690 --> 00:00:39.150
and Wall Street Journal
reporter, Evan Gershkovich

00:00:39.150 --> 00:00:41.640
have all been swept up by
the Russian legal system.

00:00:41.640 --> 00:00:45.240
- The Russian criminal justice
system is incredibly sticky.

00:00:45.240 --> 00:00:48.450
Once you're in, it's very,
very hard to get out.

00:00:48.450 --> 00:00:50.220
- [Narrator] While Griner
was released last year

00:00:50.220 --> 00:00:52.920
in a prisoner exchange,
Whelan is still imprisoned

00:00:52.920 --> 00:00:56.220
and considered wrongfully
detained by the US.

00:00:56.220 --> 00:00:58.020
Gershkovich was detained in late March

00:00:58.020 --> 00:01:00.090
and accused of espionage.

00:01:00.090 --> 00:01:01.230
Both the US government

00:01:01.230 --> 00:01:03.720
and the journal vehemently
denied the allegation

00:01:03.720 --> 00:01:05.880
and have called for his immediate release.

00:01:05.880 --> 00:01:08.370
Here's a look at how
Russia's courts have evolved

00:01:08.370 --> 00:01:10.833
but why many of its
features remain the same.

00:01:13.710 --> 00:01:16.020
Joseph Stalin presided over a period known

00:01:16.020 --> 00:01:18.420
as the Great Terror, where he used the law

00:01:18.420 --> 00:01:21.000
to eliminate opponents
and consolidate power.

00:01:21.000 --> 00:01:22.890
- There was a provision
in the criminal code,

00:01:22.890 --> 00:01:26.010
and it was a crime of
anti-Soviet behavior.

00:01:26.010 --> 00:01:27.270
And the problem was,

00:01:27.270 --> 00:01:29.490
is that there was no
crisp, clear definition

00:01:29.490 --> 00:01:31.470
of what anti-Soviet behavior was,

00:01:31.470 --> 00:01:33.570
and so it could be almost anything.

00:01:33.570 --> 00:01:35.940
- [Narrator] Historians say
millions of Soviet citizens

00:01:35.940 --> 00:01:38.100
were executed, died in labor camps,

00:01:38.100 --> 00:01:40.410
or were starved to death under Stalin.

00:01:40.410 --> 00:01:42.450
After Stalin's death, Soviet leaders

00:01:42.450 --> 00:01:45.210
like Nikita Khrushchev
abandoned mass terror.

00:01:45.210 --> 00:01:48.540
- He and the other people
who were surrounding Stalin

00:01:48.540 --> 00:01:51.630
made a decision, you know,
not written down anywhere,

00:01:51.630 --> 00:01:53.460
but a very clear decision that this kind

00:01:53.460 --> 00:01:55.680
of mass terror was not productive.

00:01:55.680 --> 00:01:56.640
- [Narrator] But that didn't mean

00:01:56.640 --> 00:01:58.320
that political arrests were over.

00:01:58.320 --> 00:02:00.300
- It just means that
they no longer took place

00:02:00.300 --> 00:02:01.620
on a mass scale.

00:02:01.620 --> 00:02:04.020
- [Narrator] Many trials
were closed to the public,

00:02:04.020 --> 00:02:05.520
and although people who were arrested

00:02:05.520 --> 00:02:06.960
were allowed to have lawyers,

00:02:06.960 --> 00:02:10.380
those lawyers had to
be approved by the KGB.

00:02:10.380 --> 00:02:12.270
When Mikhail Gorbachev came to power,

00:02:12.270 --> 00:02:15.360
he attempted to introduce
reforms to the legal system.

00:02:15.360 --> 00:02:19.410
In 1989, Gorbachev changed
the judicial selection process

00:02:19.410 --> 00:02:22.980
so that judges were no longer
picked by the Communist Party.

00:02:22.980 --> 00:02:25.020
He also instituted a jury system

00:02:25.020 --> 00:02:27.840
for the most serious criminal cases.

00:02:27.840 --> 00:02:30.420
- What he really wants is
to fix the existing system.

00:02:30.420 --> 00:02:35.190
Now, he did this in a very
kind of ham-handed way,

00:02:35.190 --> 00:02:36.720
it was destined to fail.

00:02:36.720 --> 00:02:39.210
- [Narrator] It was under his
watch that Nicholas Daniloff,

00:02:39.210 --> 00:02:41.190
a US news and World Bureau Chief,

00:02:41.190 --> 00:02:44.193
was arrested by the KGB
and accused of espionage.

00:02:45.450 --> 00:02:48.330
Daniloff had fallen into
a trap set by the KGB.

00:02:48.330 --> 00:02:51.180
Eventually, he was released
in a prisoner exchange.

00:02:51.180 --> 00:02:54.330
After the fall of the Soviet
Union, there was a rapid shift

00:02:54.330 --> 00:02:57.120
from a planned economy
to a market economy,

00:02:57.120 --> 00:02:58.470
and Russia's first president

00:02:58.470 --> 00:03:01.590
Boris Yeltsin backed a
revamp of the courts.

00:03:01.590 --> 00:03:04.560
This led to sweeping changes
within the legal profession,

00:03:04.560 --> 00:03:06.600
lawyers were free to start their own firms

00:03:06.600 --> 00:03:09.180
and specialize in certain areas of law.

00:03:09.180 --> 00:03:12.840
In 1991, a constitutional
court was created,

00:03:12.840 --> 00:03:16.290
and in 1993, Russia adopted a constitution

00:03:16.290 --> 00:03:18.930
that became the cornerstone
of the legal system.

00:03:18.930 --> 00:03:21.660
Judicial review was
introduced for the first time,

00:03:21.660 --> 00:03:23.910
meaning the constitutional
court had the power

00:03:23.910 --> 00:03:25.710
to declare acts of the legislative

00:03:25.710 --> 00:03:28.380
and executive branches
to be unconstitutional.

00:03:28.380 --> 00:03:31.470
In 1996, Russia joined
the Council of Europe,

00:03:31.470 --> 00:03:33.180
which gave Russians the right to appeal

00:03:33.180 --> 00:03:34.860
the decisions of their domestic courts

00:03:34.860 --> 00:03:37.200
to the European Court of Human Rights.

00:03:37.200 --> 00:03:39.180
However, Yeltsin still managed to go

00:03:39.180 --> 00:03:41.040
after opponents using the courts.

00:03:41.040 --> 00:03:42.420
But there was a major challenge

00:03:42.420 --> 00:03:44.760
in this era, wealthy oligarchs.

00:03:44.760 --> 00:03:46.860
- The emergence of the oligarchs brings

00:03:46.860 --> 00:03:49.860
a whole element of big
money into the courts,

00:03:49.860 --> 00:03:52.533
which is never, never helpful, right?

00:03:54.480 --> 00:03:56.010
'Cause in the Soviet period,

00:03:56.010 --> 00:03:57.690
it really wasn't that you would go in

00:03:57.690 --> 00:03:59.790
and monetarily bribe a judge.

00:03:59.790 --> 00:04:02.520
But in the 90s, that definitely happened.

00:04:02.520 --> 00:04:04.470
- [Narrator] Yeltsin set the
foundation for a division

00:04:04.470 --> 00:04:06.180
of power's approach in government,

00:04:06.180 --> 00:04:09.450
but his successor recentralized
power back to the Kremlin.

00:04:09.450 --> 00:04:11.850
In 2000, Vladimir Putin came to power

00:04:11.850 --> 00:04:13.200
as president of Russia.

00:04:13.200 --> 00:04:14.910
He forged ahead with new reforms

00:04:14.910 --> 00:04:16.470
and his supporters credited him

00:04:16.470 --> 00:04:18.480
with reigning in the power of oligarchs.

00:04:18.480 --> 00:04:20.640
He backed a new code of criminal procedure

00:04:20.640 --> 00:04:22.410
that on paper gave defendants

00:04:22.410 --> 00:04:24.510
the same rights as those in the West.

00:04:24.510 --> 00:04:27.690
However, old Soviet habits
reemerged under Putin.

00:04:27.690 --> 00:04:29.160
He passed legislation

00:04:29.160 --> 00:04:31.800
that consolidated more power in his hands.

00:04:31.800 --> 00:04:34.290
One law eliminated
direct popular elections

00:04:34.290 --> 00:04:36.450
across the country and gave him the right

00:04:36.450 --> 00:04:38.520
to appoint regional leaders in Russia.

00:04:38.520 --> 00:04:41.640
- If you control the legislature,
then what is the law?

00:04:41.640 --> 00:04:44.370
The other thing that he gets very good at

00:04:44.370 --> 00:04:48.270
is returning to this idea
of vague and overbroad laws.

00:04:48.270 --> 00:04:50.580
- [Narrator] In 2012, the
Russian punk rock band,

00:04:50.580 --> 00:04:52.830
Pussy Riot, was arrested for hooliganism

00:04:52.830 --> 00:04:55.050
after protesting Putin's growing power.

00:04:55.050 --> 00:04:59.190
- They go back to a crime of hooliganism,

00:04:59.190 --> 00:05:01.410
which is just a, you know,

00:05:01.410 --> 00:05:03.840
who knows what that means,
and it can mean anything.

00:05:03.840 --> 00:05:05.880
- [Narrator] They were
all eventually released.

00:05:05.880 --> 00:05:09.540
Then in 2020, Putin passed
several constitutional amendments

00:05:09.540 --> 00:05:12.240
that consolidated even
more power in his hands.

00:05:12.240 --> 00:05:14.010
Putin could dismiss the Prime Minister

00:05:14.010 --> 00:05:16.350
and Prosecutor General whenever he wanted.

00:05:16.350 --> 00:05:17.760
Despite decades of efforts

00:05:17.760 --> 00:05:19.710
to evolve Russia's justice system,

00:05:19.710 --> 00:05:21.990
Putin has brought back
authoritarian control

00:05:21.990 --> 00:05:23.700
and many of the elastic aspects

00:05:23.700 --> 00:05:25.860
of the Soviet era's justice system.

00:05:25.860 --> 00:05:28.260
- We're back to this old Soviet trick

00:05:28.260 --> 00:05:33.180
of finding a eternally elastic law

00:05:33.180 --> 00:05:36.153
that you can just, you
know, can catch anybody.

